# Day 14 – Fix Apache Service on All App Servers

## 📌 Task Overview

The production support team at xFusionCorp Industries received a monitoring alert indicating that the Apache service was unavailable on one of the application servers in Stratos DC.

The objective was to identify the faulty server, troubleshoot the issue, and ensure Apache was running on all three application servers using port `8088`.

## 🎯 Objectives

- Identify the application server where Apache was failing.
- Troubleshoot and resolve the Apache service issue.
- Ensure Apache is running on all three application servers.
- Verify Apache is listening on port `8088`.

## 🖥️ Infrastructure Details

| Server | Hostname | Username |
|---|---|---|
| App Server 1 | stapp01 | tony |
| App Server 2 | stapp02 | steve |
| App Server 3 | stapp03 | banner |

**Operating System:** CentOS Stream 9  
**Web Server:** Apache HTTP Server (httpd)  
**Required Port:** 8088

## 🔍 Troubleshooting and Root Cause

During investigation on App Server 1 (`stapp01`), Apache failed to start.

The error message was:

```text
(98) Address already in use
no listening sockets available
```

Further investigation revealed that the Sendmail service was occupying port `8088` on `127.0.0.1`.

Apache was also configured to listen on port `8088`, resulting in a port conflict.

## 🛠️ Solution

### 1. Identify the Port Conflict

```bash
sudo ss -tulnp
```

Check Apache listening configuration:

```bash
sudo grep -Rni '^[[:space:]]*Listen' /etc/httpd/conf /etc/httpd/conf.d/
```

### 2. Back Up Sendmail Configuration

```bash
sudo cp -a /etc/mail/sendmail.mc /etc/mail/sendmail.mc.bak
```

### 3. Correct Sendmail Port

Changed Sendmail from port `8088` to its standard SMTP port.

```bash
sudo sed -i 's/Port=8088/Port=smtp/' /etc/mail/sendmail.mc
```

Verify:

```bash
sudo grep -n 'DAEMON_OPTIONS' /etc/mail/sendmail.mc
```

### 4. Regenerate Configuration and Restart Sendmail

```bash
sudo make -C /etc/mail
sudo systemctl restart sendmail
sudo systemctl status sendmail
```

### 5. Start Apache

```bash
sudo systemctl start httpd
```

Enable Apache at boot:

```bash
sudo systemctl enable httpd
```

Check service status:

```bash
sudo systemctl status httpd
```

### 6. Verify Apache Port

```bash
sudo ss -tulnp | grep 8088
```

Expected listening port:

```text
*:8088
```

## ✅ Verification

The task requires Apache to be running on all three application servers.

| Server | Apache Service | Required Port |
|---|---|---|
| stapp01 | Active (running) | 8088 |
| stapp02 | Active (running) | 8088 |
| stapp03 | Verify using terminal evidence | 8088 |

